    "title": "Flash flooding causes headaches for drivers Wednesday - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Spencer Adkins ,\n4 hours ago\n(WOWK) — Flash flooding caused headaches on area roads Wednesday afternoon after more than two inches of rain fell on parts of the 13 News viewing area.\nAs of Wednesday night at 7:30 p.m., radar estimates show a heavy accumulation of rain from northern Lawrence County, Ohio to the east into Mason County, West Virginia. That band of more than two inches of rain stretched across Roane, Calhoun, Gilmer Upshur, Barbour and Tucker counties in West Virginia.\nDoppler radar rainfall estimates for 24 hours from 7:30 p.m. Tuesday through 7:30 p.m. Wednesday. Photos sent by Kara Fetty in Mason County, West Virginia, show water rapidly rising, and cars trying to navigate through water up and over their front bumpers.\nMason County, West Virginia, Wednesday, May 27, 2026 – Image by Kara Fetty A water rescue took place on Allison Road and Jerico Road in Mason County, according to the National Weather Service official local storm report list at about 5:20 p.m. Wednesday.\nOther locations also experienced heavy rain and even some high water such as the Scott Depot area of Putnam County in West Virginia.\nSeveral flash flood warnings were in effect across a good portion of the northern half of the 13 News viewing area. A few warnings remain in effect where 2 to 4 inches of rain are estimated to have fallen on the day.\nIf you have photos of the flooding from Wednesday, email them to news@wowktv.com\nFlash flood warnings in central West Virginia through 8:30 p.m. Wednesday, May 26, 2026 no iframe support! Drivers are reminded to never cross into an area where water covers the road.\nFrom: National Weather Service Wind damage was reported to the National Weather Service in Charleston with a tree down blocking part of Bear Wallow Road near Thomas Ridge Road in Mason County. Not far away, another large tree was reportedly blocking the entire road in the 3800 block of Ten Mile Creek Road in Mason County.\nThe rain is expected to diminish after midnight in the region and dense fog could develop. Much of the area will be rain free for days after the passage of a cold front overnight.\nWhen SEVERE WEATHER strikes, seconds matter. Download the StormTracker 13 App Copyright 2026 Nexstar Media, Inc. All rights reserved. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten, or redistributed. For the latest news, weather, sports, and streaming video, head to WOWK 13 News.",
    "summary": "That band of more than two inches of rain stretched across Roane, Calhoun, Gilmer Upshur, Barbour and Tucker counties in West Virginia. Mason County, West Virginia, Wednesday, May 27, 2026 – Image by Kara Fetty A water rescue took place on Allison Road and Jerico Road in Mason County, according to the National Weather Service official local storm report list at about 5:20 p.m. Wednesday. Other locations also experienced heavy rain and even some high water such as the Scott Depot area of Putnam County in West Virginia.",
